It seems like a silly question, but it's one of the things I've heard
people learning early on in their language acquisitions.=C2=A0 Is there an
accepted lojban phrase?

The phrase in various languages I know (and, for my fun, literal
lojban translations):
English - "Bless you" - {cesri'a do}
Russian - "=D0=B1=D1=83=D0=B4=D1=8C =D0=B7=D0=B4=D0=BE=D1=80=D0=BE=D0=B2" -=
 {ko kanro}
French - "=C3=80 tes souhaits" - {tezu'e lo do selpa'a}
Hebrew - "=D7=9C=D7=91=D7=A8=D7=99=D7=90=D7=95=D7=AA" - {tezu'e le kanro}

(If anyone knows of a better translation of the vague "to" than
{tezu'e}, I'd appreciate knowing.)

